# ANV-0065 -- a recovering reader implicitly commits a staging transaction that
# the coordinator's own read refresh would have aborted
#
# No seed, and for a different reason from ANV-0063 and ANV-0064: this one was
# found by TLC, whose input is a specification and a set of constants rather
# than a schedule. The reproduction is a .cfg file and it is checked in.
seed        = n/a -- TLC, breadth-first and exhaustive over the slice below
spec        = spec/SsiCommit.tla
config      = spec/SsiParallel.cfg
              Key = {k1, k2}   Txn = {t1, t2}   MaxTs = 6
              RefreshReadsOnPush = TRUE        (serializable)
              ParallelCommit     = TRUE        (staging records)
invariant   = NoWriteSkew
reproduce   = tools/tlc.sh SsiParallel
expect      = "Error: Invariant NoWriteSkew is violated", in about one second,
              after roughly 10,881 distinct states. The counterexample is 13
              steps and its last action is ResolveStaging.
counterexample =
              t1, t2      Begin
              t1, t2      Write (each to the key the other will read)
              t1, t2      Read  (each reads the other's key)
              t1, t2      WriteRecord  -> kStaging, full key list
              t1          Prewrite
              t1          Commit       -> kCommitted; its version is now visible
              t2          Prewrite
              --          ResolveStaging(t2): every key t2 lists carries its
                          intent, so a reader declares t2 committed. That path
                          never evaluates ReadsAreStillValid, which t1's commit
                          has just invalidated. t2's own coordinator would have
                          aborted.
control     = spec/SsiParallelSnapshot.cfg -- the same configuration at snapshot
              isolation, where there is no refresh to skip. It is clean, which
              is what makes the finding "implicit commit versus the read
              refresh" rather than "parallel commit is broken".
default     = parallel_commit is FALSE in CoordinatorOptions. Nothing ships with
              this behaviour; the drill carries the knob as a control. This is a
              finding about the design of a non-default mechanism.
status      = OPEN. Two candidate fixes, and choosing between them is a design
              decision rather than a patch: either the staging record carries
              enough for a recovering reader to perform the refresh itself, or
              implicit commit is restricted to snapshot isolation.
note        = api_visible: yes -- as write skew, in a history the engine calls
              serializable. Predicted at the end of P6 ("the measurement that
              would show it is a recovering reader racing an aborting
              coordinator") and never produced by any seed, because the sweep
              samples a much larger space than the two-transaction interleaving
              this needs.
